The [elongated emerald cut diamond](/elongated-emerald-cut-diamond) is the same stone as every other emerald cut — same step facets, same open table, same hall-of-mirrors — except one number is different on the certificate. The length-to-width ratio. That single number changes everything about how the stone reads on the hand.

A standard emerald cut sits at a 1.30–1.40 L:W ratio. It looks square-ish, nearly compact. An elongated emerald cut runs 1.40–1.65, sometimes higher.

At 1.50, a 1.5ct stone measures approximately 8.5mm long. A 2ct round brilliant is 8.1mm in diameter. The elongated emerald cut at a lower carat weight shows more length across the finger than the round at a higher weight.

That is the face-up reality most buyers never calculate before they start shopping. They compare carat weights and assume bigger carat means bigger ring. In an elongated emerald cut, that assumption is wrong.

The shape defeats it every time. And once you see the numbers, you cannot unsee them.

A complete [elongated emerald cut diamond engagement ring](/emerald-cut-diamond-engagement-rings) starts at [$4,255](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29333987?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) — a [GIA 1ct D-VS1 at $3,140](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29333987?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) in a [$1,115 Petite Four-Prong setting](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/petite-four-prong-solitaire-engagement-ring-in-18k-white-gold-item-194979?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). At the other end: a [5ct lab emerald cut ring at $9,725 total](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28857918?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) — more stone face-up than most buyers have ever seen on a hand, at a price comparable to a natural 1ct.

