## What Makes an Emerald Cut "Elongated"

![Elongated emerald cut diamond L:W ratio chart — 1.35 standard vs 1.50 elongated vs 1.65 narrow, face-up comparison by carat weight](/images/elongated-emerald-cut-diamond-lw-ratio.avif)

Every emerald cut has a length and a width. The ratio between them is printed on the GIA or IGI certificate. A stone with dimensions of 7.0mm × 5.0mm has a length-to-width ratio of 1.40.

A stone at 8.0mm × 5.0mm is 1.60. That number is the only thing that separates a standard emerald cut from an elongated one.

There is no official threshold. The industry typically considers anything above 1.40 as elongated. Below 1.35, the stone reads as nearly square — compact, geometric, more like an Asscher than an elongated rectangle.

Between 1.35 and 1.40, it reads as standard.

At 1.40 and above, the stone starts to stretch visibly across the finger. At 1.50, it dominates. At 1.60+, it is unmistakably long and rectangular, extending toward the edges of a narrow finger.

---

## The Rectangle Rule

The Rectangle Rule defines which L:W ratios work — and which cross into territory where the stone starts to look fragile, too narrow, or unstable in a setting.

**Below 1.35:** Not elongated. The stone looks compact. The long mirror reflections typical of an emerald cut are shorter and the rectangular character is less pronounced.

**1.35–1.40:** Transitional. Still reads as a standard emerald cut. Good choice for buyers who want the shape without an obviously rectangular silhouette.

**1.40–1.55:** The sweet spot. This is where most elongated emerald cut searches end. The stone reads as visibly rectangular, the long parallel step reflections are full, and the corners are proportionally stable. The stone faces up larger than its carat weight suggests.

**1.55–1.65:** Definitively elongated. The stone is long and narrow. In a four-prong solitaire, the length extends dramatically across the finger. Setting matters more at this ratio — the stone needs to be secured at all four corners without cutting into the rectangular silhouette.

**Above 1.65:** High-risk territory. The stone looks extremely narrow at the width end. The corners become proportionally smaller. Setting options are limited and the appearance of fragility increases. A few buyers specifically seek ratios in this range for a dramatic fashion effect — but it is not the standard "elongated" look most buyers are searching for.

The Rectangle Rule tells you one practical thing: **always read the L:W ratio on the certificate before buying.** Retailers describe stones as "elongated" across a wide range of actual ratios. The certificate number is the only accurate data point. A stone a retailer calls elongated at 1.38 and one at 1.58 are completely different rings on the finger.

Only the number tells the truth.

---

## The Face-Up Illusion

![Elongated emerald cut diamond face-up size vs round brilliant — 1.5ct emerald at 1.50 L:W vs 2ct round, mm measurements side by side](/images/elongated-emerald-cut-diamond-faceup.avif)

Here is the number most buyers never see until after they purchase: emerald cuts have a shallower typical depth than round brilliants. A round brilliant runs 61–63% depth. An emerald cut typically runs 58–62%, with well-cut stones often in the 59–61% range.

Shallower depth means more stone face-up. The carat weight that would sit buried in a deep round brilliant instead spreads across the surface of the emerald cut. At the same carat weight, the emerald cut shows more face-up area than the round.

Add the elongated ratio and the effect compounds. A 1.5ct round brilliant measures 7.3mm in diameter. A 1.5ct elongated emerald cut at 1.50 L:W measures approximately 8.5mm × 5.7mm.

The round's diameter is 7.3mm. The emerald cut's length is 8.5mm — 1.2mm longer than the round's entire diameter.

On the finger, that 1.2mm reads as significantly more presence. The stone extends further toward the nail edge. In photographs, it reads as a larger stone.

From arm's length, it reads as a larger ring. This is The Face-Up Illusion: the elongated emerald cut at 1.5ct reads visually larger than a 2ct round brilliant in the finger's natural orientation.

The illusion is not a trick. It is geometry. You are seeing the stone's actual face-up dimension, which in an elongated step-cut at moderate depth genuinely exceeds that of a round brilliant at a higher carat weight.

