## The 5ct Lab Window

A 5ct natural emerald cut diamond costs north of $100,000 for any GIA-certified stone in VS1+ clarity. It is not a realistic purchase for most buyers. It exists, it is extraordinary, and it is for a very small number of people.

A [5ct lab emerald cut at E-VVS1 IGI costs $8,210](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28857918?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). Add a [$1,515 Riviera Pavé WG setting](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/riviera-pave-diamond-band-engagement-ring-in-18k-white-gold-item-195326?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) and the complete ring is [$9,725](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28857918?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). This is The 5ct Lab Window.

At 5ct, an elongated emerald cut at a 1.45–1.55 L:W ratio measures approximately 13–14mm long. To put that in perspective: a 1ct round brilliant is 6.5mm in diameter. The 5ct elongated EC is more than twice as long as a 1ct round is wide.

On the finger, it extends from the base of one knuckle almost to the next.

The 5ct Lab Window exists because lab pricing does not scale linearly with carat weight the way natural pricing does. Natural diamond prices jump exponentially at size thresholds — from 1ct to 2ct, from 2ct to 3ct, and dramatically from 3ct to 5ct. Lab pricing follows supply and demand for the specific size, which means 5ct lab diamonds can be priced at similar or even lower levels than 3ct lab diamonds in some market conditions.

The E-VVS1 grade on this stone is exceptional. E color is near-colorless — correct for any metal type. VVS1 clarity in a 5ct step-cut means there is almost nothing in the stone that the naked eye or a casual loupe could identify as an inclusion.

The step-cut table at 5ct shows everything, and VVS1 ensures there is almost nothing to show.

> "A 5ct elongated emerald cut lab is one of the most dramatic rings I can recommend without breaking someone's budget. In a pavé setting, that 13mm stone from fingertip to knuckle is a genuine statement. I have shown this comparison to clients who expected to spend $15,000 on a 1.5ct natural, and they genuinely struggled to justify the natural after seeing the lab numbers side by side." — Farzana Hasan, **[Diamond Critics](https://diamondcritics.com/about-farzana/)**

