## Emerald Cut Diamond Ring 5 Carat: Natural vs Lab

The [5ct natural emerald cut diamond ring](/5-carat-emerald-cut-diamond-ring) is a different category from the lab alternative. It belongs to the investment and collector market. If you are buying a 5ct natural emerald cut, you are buying one of the rarest certified diamonds available in that shape.

The price reflects that rarity.

For buyers whose primary goal is the visual impact of a 5ct stone on the hand — the face-up size, the elongated length, the hall-of-mirrors across 13mm — the lab route delivers the identical optical experience at $9,725 complete versus $100,000+.

The decision is not about quality. At E-VVS1 in a lab stone, the quality is exceptional. The decision is about what the buyer values beyond the visual experience.

If origin matters, if long-term value retention matters, if the provenance story matters — natural is the only answer. If the goal is maximum face-up diamond at a specific budget, the lab 5ct at $9,725 complete is the clearest value proposition in certified diamond jewelry.

---

## Large Emerald Cut Diamond Ring: The 2ct–4ct Natural Range

A large emerald cut diamond ring in natural certified inventory starts becoming a serious purchase at 2ct. The certified market data for natural emerald cuts at 2ct puts GIA F-VS1 stones at approximately [$24,000–$28,000](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-search?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) for the stone alone. Add a setting and the ring total crosses $25,000.

At 2ct elongated (1.50 L:W), the stone measures approximately 9.8mm × 6.5mm. That is longer than most engagement rings at the market's most popular 1ct weight. On the hand, 9.8mm is a definitively large stone — it reads as a significant piece from normal conversation distance.

The 2ct–4ct natural range is where buyers most commonly experience the lab comparison as a genuine value shock. A [4ct D-VVS1 lab stone at $7,737](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29587315?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) in a Riviera Pavé setting at $9,252 total costs roughly one-third of a 2ct natural ring at comparable quality grades. The 4ct lab is a larger stone, higher grades (D-VVS1 vs F-VS1), and $16,000+ less expensive.

