## How to Set an Elongated Emerald Cut Diamond

The elongated emerald cut creates one specific challenge that round and cushion buyers never encounter: the corners. A longer, narrower stone at 1.50+ L:W has smaller corner facets relative to its overall size. Those corners are the most vulnerable part of any emerald cut, and they are more exposed in an elongated stone than in a standard one.

Four-prong settings are the correct choice. The prongs sit at the four corners of the stone — protecting the most exposed edges. Two prongs on each long side would leave the corners unguarded.

Four-corner prongs directly address the vulnerability.

Claw prong styles (pointed) are more secure than rounded prong styles (button) for an elongated stone because they wrap further around the corner facet. The additional contact between metal and stone at a narrow corner reduces the risk of the stone catching and chipping on impact.

Bezel settings are the most protective option — the metal surrounds the entire perimeter of the stone. For a very elongated stone (1.60+), a bezel or half-bezel setting eliminates the corner vulnerability entirely. The [Bezel Solitaire WG at $4,300](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/bezel-set-emerald-solitaire-engagement-ring-item-304632?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) is the most protective option available for an elongated emerald cut at any carat weight.

Halo settings are the setting to avoid. A circular halo conflicts directly with the rectangular outline of an elongated emerald cut. The round frame visually shortens the stone — the eye reads the halo boundary rather than the stone's actual length.

An elongated stone in a round halo appears no different from a standard emerald cut in the same halo. You lose the entire face-up advantage.

East-west settings — where the stone is rotated 90 degrees so it runs across the finger rather than along it — work exceptionally well for elongated stones. The length runs horizontally across the hand, creating a bold, modern look. At 1.50+ L:W, an east-west elongated emerald cut is dramatic in a way that no vertical-set stone at the same carat weight can match.

---

## Rectangle Emerald Cut Diamond: How to Read the Certificate

The terms "rectangle emerald cut diamond" and "elongated emerald cut diamond" describe the same shape. Buyers use both to search for the same product. What they are really describing is the L:W ratio — the degree to which the stone looks rectangular rather than nearly square.

The GIA certificate lists length and width in millimeters under the Measurements section. The format is: Length × Width × Depth in millimeters, always listed Length first (the long dimension). Divide length by width to calculate the L:W ratio yourself.

Do not rely on retailers' descriptions of stones as "elongated" — confirm the ratio on the certificate.

A stone listed as 8.50 × 5.70 × 3.42mm has an L:W ratio of 8.50 ÷ 5.70 = 1.49. That is squarely in the sweet spot range of 1.45–1.55. A stone listed as 9.20 × 5.50 × 3.40mm is 9.20 ÷ 5.50 = 1.67.

That is above the standard elongated range — it will look narrow and very long.

Always calculate. Always verify.

---

## Which Diamond Shape Has the Largest Face-Up Per Carat?

The question "which diamond shapes face up largest per carat" has a direct answer: **marquise first, oval second, elongated emerald cut third, round brilliant fourth.**

The marquise has the highest face-up area of any shape because its pointed ends extend further from the center than any other outline. At 1ct, a marquise with a 2.00 L:W ratio can show 43+ mm² of face-up area versus a round brilliant's 33 mm².

The oval comes second. Its rounded outline at a 1.35–1.50 L:W shows more face-up than a round at the same carat weight.

The elongated emerald cut comes third — and in one specific way surpasses the oval. The emerald cut's open step-cut table reads as a single unbroken expanse of reflected light rather than the collection of brilliant facets that characterize an oval or marquise. That continuous surface reads as more diamond visually, even if the measured face-up area is similar or slightly less than an oval.

The round brilliant is the least efficient shape for face-up area per carat among major shapes. It trades face-up size for optical performance — the round brilliant's 57 facets produce more light return and scintillation than a step cut. That is a trade buyers make consciously based on what they value more: sparkle or size.

For buyers who prioritize visual face-up presence over scintillation, the elongated emerald cut is the third-largest face-up shape and the most architecturally distinctive.

